      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Coronary artery disease, an inflammatory disease, may be caused by infection. We investigated whether the antibiotic clarithromycin would reduce morbidity and mortality in patients with acute non-Q-wave coronary syndrome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND RESULTS",
          "content": "Altogether, 148 patients with acute non-Q-wave infarction or unstable angina were randomly assigned to receive double-blind treatment with either clarithromycin or placebo for 3 months. The primary end point was a composite of death, myocardial infarction, or unstable angina during treatment; the secondary end point was occurrence of any cardiovascular event during the entire follow-up period (average 555 days, range 138 to 924 days). There was a trend toward fewer patients meeting primary end-point criteria in the clarithromycin group than in the placebo group (11 versus 19 patients, respectively; risk ratio 0.54, 95% CI 0.25 to 1.14; P=0.10). By the end of the entire follow-up, 16 patients in the clarithromycin group and 27 in the placebo group had experienced a cardiovascular event (risk ratio 0.49, 95% CI 0.26 to 0.92; P=0.03)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Clarithromycin appears to reduce the risk of ischemic cardiovascular events in patients presenting with acute non-Q-wave infarction or unstable angina. No signs of this effect diminishing were observed during follow-up."
        }
      ]},
